# Copy this file to .env for local development (`cp .env.example .env`), or
# set these as Environment Variables in the Vercel project settings for
# deployment. Vite only exposes variables prefixed with VITE_ to the client
# bundle — anything set here is effectively public once built, so only
# non-secret values belong in this file / on Vercel.

# Google OAuth2 Client ID (Google Cloud Console -> Credentials -> OAuth 2.0
# Client ID, Application type: Web). This is a public identifier, not a
# secret — Google Identity Services enforces access via the "Authorized
# JavaScript origins" allowlist you configure alongside it, not via secrecy.
# When set, the app uses it automatically and the manual "Google OAuth2
# Client ID" field in Settings is hidden.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AI provider API keys (Gemini, Claude, Ollama Cloud) are NOT set here and
# NOT set on Vercel. This is a static site with no server of its own, so any
# VITE_-prefixed key would be baked into the public JS bundle and readable by
# anyone who opens dev tools on the deployed site. Instead, those keys are
# set as Supabase Edge Function secrets, which only run server-side:
#
#   Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Edge Functions -> Secrets
#     GEMINI_API_KEY        (used by the notes_gemini-proxy function)
#     CLAUDE_API_KEY        (used by the notes_claude-proxy function)
#     OLLAMA_CLOUD_API_KEY  (used by the notes_ollama-proxy function)
#
# OpenAI is the one provider that still takes a key from the browser (Settings
# page, stored in that browser's localStorage) — it isn't proxied.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
